— ANSWER —
The Prayer of Manasseh — 'I bow the knee of mine heart' — is a repentance prayer preserved in the KJV 1611 Apocrypha, spoken by King Manasseh of Judah after his captivity in Babylon.

AI Bible Gospels reads the Prayer of Manasseh from the KJV 1611, where Judah's king pleads: 'Now therefore I bow the knee of mine heart, beseeching thee of grace.' The prayer is a model of repentance — acknowledging iniquity, asking not to be destroyed, confessing the Most High as the God of them that repent.

— Q&A —
Q: What is the Prayer of Manasseh in the KJV Bible?
A: A repentance prayer by King Manasseh of Judah, preserved in the 1611 KJV Apocrypha. It opens, 'Now therefore I bow the knee of mine heart, beseeching thee of grace,' and confesses sin before the God of them that repent.

Q: Why was the Prayer of Manasseh removed from modern Bibles?
A: It was included in the 1611 King James Version Apocrypha but stripped from later printings in the 19th century. The text remains scripture in the original KJV and in Eastern Orthodox canon.

